from core import L
L(7,'processes','System calls, processes, threads and IPC','System calls;Processes;Threads;Inter-process communication','C memory and instruction execution.',
'A running program needs an execution context and protected resources. Processes give resource isolation; threads share much of that context while scheduling independently. Kernel entry is controlled because ordinary programs must not directly manipulate every device or other process.',
'''System calls and process states::A system call enters privileged kernel handling through an architecture-defined trap mechanism. The kernel validates arguments and performs protected work. A process can be running, ready or blocked; blocked means waiting for an event, not merely waiting for CPU. A context switch saves and restores execution state and is distinct from any user-to-kernel mode transition.
||Threads and creation::Threads in one process share an address space and open resources but normally have separate stacks and register contexts. A process creation mechanism such as fork copies a logical process context; modern implementations may initially share pages via copy-on-write. Different processes do not ordinarily share arbitrary writable memory unless explicitly arranged.
||IPC mechanisms::Pipes and message queues move data with OS coordination. Shared memory lets processes access a common region and can reduce copying but requires synchronization. Signals provide notifications rather than general large-data transfer. Blocking calls suspend a caller until conditions are met; nonblocking calls return immediately with status. Choose the mechanism based on data size, synchronization and isolation needs.''',
'''Distinguish ready from blocked::A process waiting for the scheduler after exhausting a time slice is ready.~A process waiting for disk completion is blocked.~When disk completion arrives, it usually becomes ready before the scheduler runs it again.
||Two threads modify a shared global::Both threads refer to the same process-global object.~Each still has its own local stack variables and instruction pointer.~Sharing the global creates a synchronization responsibility; having two stacks does not isolate global state.''',
'A mode switch is not necessarily a process switch.||Shared memory does not automatically synchronize access.',
'Process isolates resources; threads share address space.||Ready waits for CPU; blocked waits for an event.',
[('Four threads share one process. How many ordinary thread stacks are needed?',4,'Each thread needs its own active call context.','One stack per thread gives four stacks.'),('A single CPU runs one thread at an instant in this simplified model. How many threads can be running simultaneously?',1,'Ignore multicore hardware in the stated model.','A single execution core runs one thread at a time.')])
L(7,'synchronization','Concurrency, mutexes and semaphores','Concurrency;Synchronization;Critical section;Semaphore;Mutex','Processes, threads and interleaving.',
'Concurrency allows operations to overlap in time even on one CPU. If a shared update consists of separate read and write steps, scheduling can interleave those steps and lose information. Correct synchronization constrains interleavings, not just the final line of source code.',
'''Race conditions and critical sections::A data race involves unsynchronized conflicting accesses under the applicable memory model; broader race conditions make correctness depend on timing. A critical section protects shared invariants. Mutual exclusion ensures at most one entrant, while progress and bounded waiting address liveness. Declaring an object volatile is not a substitute for atomicity or a lock.
||Mutex and semaphore::A mutex gives exclusive ownership around a protected region. A semaphore maintains a count with atomic wait and signal operations. wait decrements when available or blocks; signal returns a permit or wakes a waiter under the implementation model. A binary semaphore can coordinate single access but ownership semantics need not match a mutex. Counting semaphores manage multiple identical resources.
||Producer–consumer::For a capacity-N buffer, use empty=N, full=0 and a mutex for buffer manipulation. Producer waits for empty then locks, inserts, unlocks and signals full; consumer symmetrically waits for full and later signals empty. Waiting for availability while holding the buffer mutex can block the other side from making progress.''',
'''Show a lost update for shared x=0::Thread A reads 0; thread B reads 0.~A computes and stores 1; B also computes and stores 1.~Two increments finish with x=1 rather than 2. Locking the whole read-modify-write operation prevents that interleaving.
||Manage three identical devices::Initialize semaphore count to 3.~Three successful waits consume all permits, leaving 0.~A fourth requester must block until some owner signals, restoring a permit.''',
'Protect the whole invariant update, not only one read or store.||Incorrect lock order can introduce deadlock.',
'Mutual exclusion protects safety; progress/bounded waiting protect liveness.||Counting semaphore permits model resource capacity.',
[('A semaphore starts at 5 and three waits succeed without signals. What is its remaining count?',2,'Each successful wait consumes one permit.','5−3=2.'),('Two correctly locked increments start from x=7. What is the final value?',9,'Both increments take effect serially.','7+1+1=9.')])
L(7,'deadlock','Deadlock, avoidance and safe states','Deadlock;Coffman conditions;Bankers algorithm','Processes, resources and synchronization.',
'Deadlock is a circular dependency in which participants cannot proceed. A state can be unsafe without already being deadlocked: unsafe means future declared resource demands might prevent a guaranteed completion order.',
'''Necessary conditions::The standard Coffman conditions are mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no forced resource preemption and circular wait. All are necessary in the usual resource-allocation model. Prevention deliberately breaks one, for example imposing a global resource-acquisition order to rule out circular waiting.
||Avoidance with Banker::Each process declares its maximum demand. Need=Max−Allocation, and Available is the currently free vector. A safety test repeatedly finds a process with Need≤Work, assumes it completes, and adds its Allocation back to Work. If all can be removed, the resulting sequence certifies safety. A resource request is tentatively granted only if the new state passes the test.
||Detection and model limits::In a single-instance resource graph, a cycle corresponds to deadlock; with multiple instances, a cycle need not be sufficient. Detection examines actual allocations and outstanding requests rather than maximum future claims. Recovery can terminate or roll back processes or preempt suitable resources. Starvation is indefinite denial to a participant while others may still progress, which differs from deadlock.''',
'''Check safety: Available=1, P1 allocation=1/max=2, P2 allocation=1/max=3::Needs are 1 and 2.~P1 can finish using the available unit, releasing its allocation and making Work=2.~P2 can then finish. Sequence P1,P2 certifies safety.
||Impose lock order::Every thread must acquire lock A before lock B.~A thread holding B cannot wait for A because that acquisition order is disallowed.~This removes a possible A↔B circular wait, though other correctness concerns still need analysis.''',
'Unsafe is not synonymous with already deadlocked.||A cycle is not sufficient in every multiple-instance resource graph.',
'Need=Max−Allocation.||Safety test simulates finishing processes and releasing allocations.',
[('A process has Max=7 and Allocation=3 for one resource. What is Need?',4,'Subtract what it already holds.','7−3=4 additional units.'),('Work=2 and a simulated completed process releases Allocation=3. What is the new Work?',5,'Return the allocation.','2+3=5 available units.')])
L(7,'scheduling','CPU and I/O scheduling','CPU scheduling;I/O scheduling;FCFS;SJF;Round robin;SCAN','Processes, queues and timelines.',
'Scheduling chooses which waiting work receives a resource next. A schedule should be traced on a time axis using arrival times and preemption rules. Waiting, response and turnaround times describe different experiences and should not be interchanged.',
'''CPU metrics::Turnaround=completion−arrival. In the single CPU-burst model, waiting=turnaround−burst. Response time measures first execution−arrival. FCFS serves arrival order and may create a convoy behind a long job. Nonpreemptive SJF picks the shortest available next burst; shortest-remaining-time-first is its preemptive variant. Future arrivals cannot be scheduled before they exist.
||Round-robin::Run each ready process for at most quantum q, then requeue if unfinished. Small q improves opportunities for responsiveness but raises context-switch overhead; very large q approaches FCFS. At simultaneous arrivals/completions define the queue tie convention. Priority scheduling may starve low-priority work; aging can increase priority over time.
||Disk scheduling::FCFS follows request order. SSTF picks the nearest requested cylinder, potentially starving distant requests. SCAN moves in a direction and reverses, while LOOK reverses at the last request rather than physical end. C-SCAN serves one direction with a return movement. Total head travel depends on the initial position, direction and whether endpoint movement counts.''',
'''FCFS with both arrivals at 0 and bursts 3,5::Run P1 from 0 to 3 and P2 from 3 to 8.~Waiting times are 0 and 3; mean=1.5.~Turnaround times are 3 and 8; mean=5.5. The metrics differ despite the same schedule.
||SSTF head at 50 with requests 10,55,80::Closest is 55, movement 5.~From 55, closest remaining is 80, movement 25.~Finish at 10, movement 70. Total movement=5+25+70=100 cylinders.''',
'Consider only processes that have arrived.||Disk LOOK and SCAN endpoint rules differ.',
'Turnaround=completion−arrival.||Waiting=turnaround−burst in the stated CPU-only model.',
[('Arrival=2, completion=12 and CPU burst=6. What is waiting time?',4,'Compute turnaround first.','Turnaround=12−2=10; waiting=10−6=4.'),('A disk head moves from 40 to 65 then to 20. What is total cylinder travel?',70,'Add absolute differences.','|65−40|+|20−65|=25+45=70.')])
L(7,'virtual-files','Memory management, virtual memory and file systems','Memory management;Virtual memory;Paging;TLB;Page replacement;File systems','Addresses, caches and disk storage.',
'Virtual memory separates a program’s address space from physical placement. A translation may be missing from the TLB even when the page is resident; a page fault instead means the required resident mapping cannot complete normally and OS handling is needed.',
'''Paging and translation::Split a virtual address into page number and offset. The page table maps the page number to a physical frame; offset is unchanged. A TLB caches translations, not ordinarily the data itself. Page-table size depends on page count and entry size. Larger pages reduce entry count but can increase internal fragmentation. Segmentation models variable-size logical regions and has different allocation trade-offs.
||Replacement and faults::Demand paging loads pages when first needed or after eviction. FIFO removes the oldest loaded page, LRU the least recently used, and optimal the page whose next use is farthest in the future. Optimal needs future knowledge and is a benchmark. FIFO can exhibit Belady’s anomaly; LRU is a stack algorithm and does not. Too few frames for active working sets can cause thrashing.
||File organization::Directories map names to metadata or file identifiers. Contiguous allocation supports simple sequential/random positioning but complicates growth; linked allocation traverses pointers; indexed allocation stores block pointers in an index structure. Free-space maps, access permissions and crash consistency are separate responsibilities. An inode-like record can point to direct and indirect block addresses.''',
'''Translate virtual address 5000 with 4 KiB pages::Page size=4096; page number=floor(5000/4096)=1.~Offset=5000−4096=904.~If page 1 maps to frame 7, physical address=7×4096+904=29576.
||FIFO with 2 frames and references 1,2,1,3::1 faults and loads; 2 faults and loads.~The second reference to 1 hits but does not change FIFO age.~3 faults and evicts 1, the oldest loaded page. Total faults=3; LRU would evict 2 instead.''',
'A TLB miss is not automatically a page fault.||FIFO age is load order, not last access.',
'Offset bits=log₂page bytes.||Physical address=frame×page size+offset.||File allocation affects lookup/growth costs.',
[('A 4 KiB page needs how many offset bits?',12,'4096 is a power of two.','4096=2¹², requiring 12 bits.'),('With page size 1024 bytes, what is the offset of virtual address 2500?',452,'Take the remainder modulo page size.','2500=2×1024+452, so offset=452.')])
L(8,'er-relations','ER modelling, relational schemas and integrity','ER-model;Relational model;Integrity constraints;Keys','Sets and relations.',
'A database model captures which facts exist and how they relate. ER diagrams express entities and relationships at a conceptual level; relational schemas encode them as tables with keys and constraints that prevent invalid states.',
'''ER concepts and mapping::Entities have identifying attributes; relationships link entities with cardinality and participation constraints. A one-to-many relationship is often represented by a foreign key on the many side. A many-to-many relationship becomes its own relation with foreign keys to both sides and any relationship attributes. Weak entities require an owner key plus their partial identifier.
||Relational keys::A superkey uniquely determines a tuple; a candidate key is a minimal superkey. Choose one candidate as primary key. Keys can span multiple attributes, and additional unique constraints can express alternate keys. Relations in the mathematical model are sets of tuples, unlike SQL’s default duplicate-preserving query results.
||Integrity::Domain constraints restrict value types/ranges; entity integrity requires primary-key values not be NULL; referential integrity requires a non-NULL foreign key to match an allowed referenced key. Deleting a referenced tuple may be rejected or trigger defined cascading/set-null actions. A foreign key need not be unique on the referencing side.''',
'''Map students taking courses::Student has key student_id; Course has key course_id.~Because many students take many courses, create Enrollment(student_id,course_id,grade).~The pair can be its key if each student takes each course once, and both identifiers are foreign keys. Repeated offerings would need an additional term/attempt attribute.
||Distinguish superkey from candidate key::If student_id alone identifies a row, {student_id,name} also identifies it.~The larger set is a superkey.~It is not a candidate key because removing name still preserves uniqueness.''',
'A candidate key is minimal by inclusion, not simply smallest-looking.||A foreign key is not necessarily a unique identifier.',
'M:N relationship→separate relation.||Candidate key=minimal superkey.',
[('A many-to-many Enrollment row uses student_id and course_id as its composite key. How many attributes are in that key?',2,'Count the named key components.','The pair contains two attributes.'),('A relation has 3 candidate keys, and one is selected as primary. How many alternate candidate keys remain?',2,'Remove the selected primary from the count.','3−1=2 alternate keys.')])
L(8,'queries','Relational algebra, tuple calculus and SQL','Relational algebra;Tuple calculus;SQL;Joins;Grouping','Relational schemas and sets.',
'A query describes a derived relation. Algebra describes operations to produce it, while calculus describes a logical condition on desired tuples. SQL uses related ideas but adds practical semantics for duplicates, NULL and grouping.',
'''Algebra and calculus::Selection σ filters tuples; projection π keeps named columns and removes duplicates in set-based algebra. Cartesian product pairs every tuple; a join filters combinations by a condition. Union and difference require compatible schemas. Tuple relational calculus uses predicates on tuples; safe/range-restricted expressions ensure finite, domain-independent results.
||SQL order and joins::Conceptually FROM/JOIN establishes sources, WHERE filters rows, GROUP BY forms groups, HAVING filters groups, SELECT chooses expressions, and ORDER BY orders results. An inner join requires a match; an outer join also retains unmatched rows with NULL-filled columns. Filtering an outer-joined table in WHERE can discard the unmatched rows and change the intended semantics.
||Duplicates, aggregates and NULL::SELECT ordinarily keeps duplicates; DISTINCT removes them. COUNT(*) counts rows, while COUNT(column) counts non-NULL values. SUM/AVG ordinarily ignore NULL inputs. Comparisons involving NULL usually yield UNKNOWN; WHERE retains only TRUE. Use IS NULL rather than =NULL. SQL OR/AND follows three-valued logic in such cases.''',
'''Count students per department with at least three students::Group Student rows by dept.~Use SELECT dept, COUNT(*) FROM Student GROUP BY dept HAVING COUNT(*)>=3.~The group filter belongs in HAVING; WHERE cannot directly filter by the completed aggregate count.
||COUNT semantics for values [7,NULL,9]::There are three rows, so COUNT(*)=3.~Only two have non-NULL column values, so COUNT(value)=2.~AVG(value)=(7+9)/2=8, not 16/3.''',
'Projection in relational algebra eliminates duplicates; SQL SELECT does not by default.||NULL is not an ordinary comparable value.',
'WHERE filters rows; HAVING filters groups.||COUNT(*) includes all rows.',
[('A table has 5 rows, two NULL in column x. What is COUNT(x)?',3,'Exclude NULL values for the named column count.','5−2=3 non-NULL values.'),('Relations with 3 and 4 tuples have how many tuples in their Cartesian product?',12,'Every tuple pairs with every tuple.','3×4=12 pairs.')])
L(8,'normalization','Functional dependencies and normal forms','Normal forms;Functional dependencies;Closure;2NF;3NF;BCNF','Keys and relational schemas.',
'Normalization controls redundancy caused by dependencies. A dependency is a rule about every valid database state, not a coincidence in one sample table. Begin by computing keys; normal-form tests depend on superkeys and prime attributes.',
'''Closure and inference::X→Y says equal values on X force equal values on Y. Attribute closure X⁺ starts with X and repeatedly adds RHS attributes whose LHS is already present. If X⁺ contains the whole schema, X is a superkey; remove redundant attributes to test minimality. Armstrong’s reflexivity, augmentation and transitivity support sound dependency inference.
||2NF, 3NF and BCNF::After atomic-value 1NF, 2NF rules out dependence of a nonprime attribute on a proper subset of a candidate key. In 3NF, every nontrivial X→A has X a superkey or A prime, meaning in some candidate key. BCNF drops the prime-attribute exception and requires every nontrivial determinant to be a superkey. BCNF implies 3NF, but not conversely.
||Decomposition::Lossless join means decomposed relations reconstruct exactly the original valid relation, with no spurious tuples. For a binary decomposition into R1,R2, their intersection determining all of R1 or all of R2 under F⁺ is the usual lossless criterion. Dependency preservation permits checking original dependencies without joining. These are separate properties; a BCNF decomposition can lose dependency preservation.''',
'''Normalize R(A,B,C) with A→B and B→C::A⁺={A,B,C}, so A is a candidate key.~B⁺={B,C}; B is not a superkey, and C is nonprime, so B→C violates 3NF and BCNF.~Decompose into AB and BC. Shared B determines BC, so the binary decomposition is lossless and preserves the given dependencies.
||Compute closure with A→B, BC→D and A→C::Start A⁺={A}.~Add B and C from the two A dependencies.~Now BC is present, so add D. A⁺={A,B,C,D}.''',
'Sample rows do not prove a dependency holds universally.||Lossless and dependency-preserving are different tests.',
'3NF: superkey determinant or prime RHS.||BCNF: every nontrivial determinant is a superkey.',
[('For A→B,B→C on R(A,B,C), how many attributes are in A⁺?',3,'Apply dependencies until no new attribute appears.','A adds B, which adds C; closure size is 3.'),('How many attributes are in B⁺ for the same dependencies?',2,'A cannot be inferred from B.','B⁺={B,C}, size 2.')])
L(8,'storage-index','File organization and B/B+ tree indexing','File organization;Indexing;B trees;B+ trees','Trees, pages and disk I/O.',
'An index is an auxiliary route to data, not the data model itself. On-disk indexes use high branching factors to reduce expensive page reads. Equality and range queries can prefer different organizations.',
'''File layout::Heap files place records in convenient free space and favour insertion without key order. Sorted files favour ordered scans but make insertion maintenance costly. Hash organization targets equality buckets and generally does not preserve ordering. A clustered organization aligns data placement with index order; a secondary index may require scattered data-page fetches.
||B-tree family::B and B+ trees remain balanced and store many separator keys per page. In a common B+ design, internal nodes guide search while data entries are at leaves, which are linked for range scans. Definitions of “order” vary: read whether it means maximum children, maximum keys or minimum occupancy. Splits, redistribution and merges preserve occupancy and balance.
||Cost reasoning::With fanout f and many entries, height grows roughly logarithmically in f. Point search follows one root-to-leaf path; a range search locates its starting leaf then walks linked leaves. Dense indexes have an entry for every relevant key/record convention; sparse indexes have selected entries and ordinarily require ordered data. Include data-page reads separately when an index does not contain the required record.''',
'''Estimate maximum leaves for a B+ tree with 3 levels and fanout 4::The root is level 1 and can point to 4 internal nodes.~Each internal node can point to 4 leaves.~Maximum leaves=4×4=16, excluding leaf entry capacity from this count.
||Range query using a B+ index::Search down to the leaf containing the first key at least the lower bound.~Scan that leaf and follow next-leaf links.~Stop once the upper bound is exceeded; do not restart root search for each successive key.''',
'B-tree “order” conventions are not universal.||An index-only search differs from an index lookup plus record fetch.',
'High fanout reduces height.||B+ leaf links support range scans; hashing favours equality.',
[('A root with fanout 5 points directly to leaves. What is the maximum number of leaves?',5,'One child pointer selects one leaf.','At most 5 leaves.'),('A disk page of 4096 bytes stores fixed-size 128-byte records without overhead. How many fit?',32,'Divide page bytes by record bytes.','4096/128=32 records.')])
L(8,'transactions','Transactions, serializability and concurrency control','Transactions;Concurrency control;ACID;Serializability;Two phase locking','SQL updates, graphs and synchronization.',
'Transactions group operations into a unit whose intermediate effects must be controlled. Concurrency can improve throughput, but interleavings must preserve an allowed relationship to serial execution and support recovery when work fails.',
'''ACID and anomalies::Atomicity gives all-or-nothing effects; consistency preserves declared invariants when transactions themselves are correct; isolation controls interference; durability preserves committed effects after recovery. Lost updates overwrite another transaction’s changes. Dirty reads observe uncommitted writes. Nonrepeatable reads and phantoms involve changing rows or predicate result sets across reads.
||Conflict serializability::Two operations conflict if they are in different transactions, access the same item, and at least one writes. Add Ti→Tj when Ti’s conflicting operation precedes Tj’s. The schedule is conflict serializable iff this precedence graph is acyclic; a topological order gives an equivalent serial ordering. Read-read pairs create no edge. Conflict serializability is stronger than some other semantic notions of serializability.
||Locks and recovery::Two-phase locking first acquires locks, then releases without acquiring new ones. It guarantees conflict serializability under the standard model but can deadlock. Strict variants retain required exclusive locks until commit/abort to prevent cascading effects. Write-ahead logging requires appropriate log records be durable before related dirty data pages are written. Recovery may redo committed work and undo uncommitted work according to its logging scheme.''',
'''Analyse W1(X),R2(X),W2(Y),R1(Y)::W1(X) before R2(X) adds T1→T2.~W2(Y) before R1(Y) adds T2→T1.~The graph has a cycle, so the schedule is not conflict serializable.
||Analyse R1(X),R2(X),W1(Y),R2(Y)::The reads of X do not conflict.~W1(Y) before R2(Y) creates only T1→T2.~The graph is acyclic; serial order T1,T2 is conflict-equivalent.''',
'Read-read overlap does not create a conflict.||Two-phase locking can still deadlock.',
'Acyclic precedence graph ⇔ conflict serializable.||WAL orders logging before corresponding data writes.',
[('Two transactions perform only reads of the same item. How many conflict edges are created?',0,'At least one operation must write.','Read-read pairs do not conflict, so zero edges.'),('A precedence graph contains T1→T2 and T2→T1. How many vertices are in this directed cycle?',2,'Follow the two opposite edges.','The cycle involves the two transactions.')])
